      children: "Osho reads this chapter as the first real taste of meditation in the Gita. The atman is not a dogma to be believed; it is a dimension to be discovered. When Krishna says \"the wise grieve not,\" he is not scolding — he is pointing. Grief is identification with the form; freedom is the discovery of the formless. And the famous command — \"you have a right to action alone, never to its fruits\" — is not a morality lesson; it is a method of living that burns the ego from inside. Do the act fully, and let the result fall where it may. That is the whole of karma yoga, and it is born in this chapter of wisdom."
